.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt FSCK_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fsck_minixfs
.Nd check and repair a MINIX file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl lwy
.Op Fl e Cm 0 | 1
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
checks the MINIX file system in
.Ar image ,
of any version and byte order, or of Minix-vmd; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
It checks:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the super block: a maximum file size that makes sense, an image that
holds the whole file system, where the size of a device can be told,
and the errors that Linux records in the state field of V1 and V2;
.It
directory entries: inode numbers in range, entries that name a free
inode or one of no valid type, names that are empty or hold
.Ql / ,
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&..
first and naming the directory and its parent, and directories that
more than one directory lists;
.It
inodes: a size that the zones reach, directories a whole number of
entries long, zone numbers inside the data area, zones that two files
share, device files with zone numbers besides the device number, and
symbolic links that are empty, longer than Linux writes or cut short by
a NUL byte;
.It
link counts against the entries that name each inode, unreferenced
inodes (inodes in use that no directory names), both bit maps, and bit 0 of each map, which is
never used but must be set.
.El
.Pp
Neither the depth of the directory tree nor loops of directories stop
the check.
Each problem is printed on a line of its own, then a summary of the
inodes and zones in use and the problems found.
A file system that is not marked clean is noted, but is not a problem in
itself: in V1 and V2, MINIX leaves zero the word in which Linux keeps the
mark.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
the image is only read.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl y
Repair each problem where it can be: bad entries are removed,
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&..
are pointed where they belong or put back, moving an entry that holds
their place and giving the directory a zone if it has none; zone numbers
outside the data area and the second use of a zone are cleared; sizes
are cut to what the zones reach, to whole entries and to the text of a
symbolic link.
Link counts and bit maps are corrected, unreferenced inodes are freed,
and the maximum file size in the super block is corrected.
A root that is not a directory and an image shorter than the file
system are left.
Each problem is followed by
.Ql (repaired)
or
.Ql (not repaired) .
The image is then checked again and marked clean if nothing is left \(em
in V1 and V2 as Linux marks it, in V3 with the clean flag of MINIX 3,
which otherwise mounts the file system read-only \(em or marked as
having errors if problems remain.
The image is locked with
.Xr fcntl 2
while it is written, and one that another program holds is refused.
.It Fl l
With
.Fl y ,
link unreferenced inodes into
.Pa /lost+found
as
.Ql #
and their inode number, trees below directories and all, rather than
freeing them;
.Pa /lost+found
is made if it is not there.
MINIX and Linux have no
.Pa /lost+found ,
and their fsck frees such inodes, which is why this is not the default.
.It Fl e Cm 0 | 1
Check that the bits of each map past the last inode or zone are 0 or 1,
and with
.Fl y
set them so.
The mkfs of MINIX leaves them clear and that of Linux sets them, so
neither is wrong unless this says which to expect.
.It Fl w
Note, as warnings that are not problems, what the fsck of MINIX 3 warns
about: map blocks beyond what the maps need, a first data zone later
than the inode table allows, a maximum file size other than the one
MINIX works out, and zones of more than 256 blocks.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Check, and repair in place, an image that holds the file system in the
tracks of one side of a disk only, as for
.Xr minixfs 1 ;
the other side is left alone.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 if the file system is consistent, after the repairs with
.Fl y ;
1 if problems remain; 2 for a usage error; and 3 if the image cannot be
checked at all.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fsck_minixfs root.img
$ fsck_minixfs -y -l usr.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr tunefs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
The flex directories of Minix-vmd are checked, but
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&..
are not put back there, nor is
.Pa /lost+found
made in them.
